00:00:09.350 --> 00:00:10.825
by Google Cloud Platform.

00:00:10.825 --> 00:00:12.200
My name is Chad
Jennings, and I'm

00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:13.554
a product manager for BigQuery.

00:00:13.554 --> 00:00:14.720
SOLEIL KELLEY: Thanks, Chad.

00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:15.530
My name is Soleil Kelley.

00:00:15.530 --> 00:00:17.780
I'm a product marketer on
the data analytics team

00:00:17.780 --> 00:00:19.610
here at Google Cloud.

00:00:19.610 --> 00:00:22.460
And today, we're going to
talk about geospatial analysis

00:00:22.460 --> 00:00:26.180
and BigQuery, in particular,
doing that with BigQuery GIS.

00:00:26.180 --> 00:00:28.920
Do note, you can ask questions
at any time on the platform.

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:32.840
We have Googlers on standby
to answer your questions.

00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:35.060
So our goal today is
really a couple-fold.

00:00:35.060 --> 00:00:37.970
We want to, A, introduce you
to the BigQuery GIS service

00:00:37.970 --> 00:00:40.790
if you're not yet familiar
with that and, B, get you up

00:00:40.790 --> 00:00:43.850
and running so you can
add GIS into your toolbox

00:00:43.850 --> 00:00:44.940
as an analyst.

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:47.400
And let's jump right in.

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:51.740
So spatial context really
matters from our perspective.

00:00:51.740 --> 00:00:55.262
Maps provide this really
unique type of context.

00:00:55.262 --> 00:00:57.720
And when we think of context,
think of the who, what, when,

00:00:57.720 --> 00:00:59.330
where, whys, and hows.

00:00:59.330 --> 00:01:02.180
In particular, that where--

00:01:02.180 --> 00:01:04.699
when you add your data,
and you put it on a map,

00:01:04.699 --> 00:01:05.990
it just instantly becomes real.

00:01:05.990 --> 00:01:07.340
It becomes relatable as a human.

00:01:07.340 --> 00:01:09.798
And that's really powerful to
help you make better business

00:01:09.798 --> 00:01:11.300
decisions.

00:01:11.300 --> 00:01:14.870
It, second of all, gives
you this rich extra set

00:01:14.870 --> 00:01:16.374
of data at your disposal.

00:01:16.374 --> 00:01:18.290
And there's a nice benefit
there because, when

00:01:18.290 --> 00:01:21.050
you put your data on that map,
and you join with other things,

00:01:21.050 --> 00:01:24.297
you instantly have context
in physical space, right?

00:01:24.297 --> 00:01:25.880
Those maps connect
that physical space

00:01:25.880 --> 00:01:28.190
to that various, sometimes
intangible space,

00:01:28.190 --> 00:01:32.060
of data analysis on a computer
connected to the cloud, right?

00:01:32.060 --> 00:01:34.910
And so yeah, maps really matter
from the human perspective

00:01:34.910 --> 00:01:36.630
and also from the
business perspective.

00:01:36.630 --> 00:01:40.070
So take, for example,
this map in Denver.

00:01:40.070 --> 00:01:42.890
I might be a retailer in this
particular location in Denver.

00:01:42.890 --> 00:01:46.520
And I would want to analyze
where my customers are

00:01:46.520 --> 00:01:49.400
or the different demographics
from different neighborhoods

00:01:49.400 --> 00:01:51.170
in the Denver area.

00:01:51.170 --> 00:01:53.780
And maybe I'm considering
opening up a new store

00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:55.100
in a different neighborhood.

00:01:55.100 --> 00:01:58.910
I would also want to factor in
other competitors in the area

00:01:58.910 --> 00:02:01.630
and think about different
physical geographies that are

00:02:01.630 --> 00:02:02.880
present in this space as well.

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:05.510
And when I actually overlay
my demographic analysis

00:02:05.510 --> 00:02:09.220
on top of the map, as opposed to
just looking at tables of data,

00:02:09.220 --> 00:02:10.850
I instantly just
have more context.

00:02:10.850 --> 00:02:12.350
I can see where the
freeways are that might

00:02:12.350 --> 00:02:14.766
be gridlocked during that time
I want my customers to come

00:02:14.766 --> 00:02:15.290
to my store.

00:02:15.290 --> 00:02:17.480
I can see physical,
geographic boundaries,

00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:19.400
like bodies of water
or mountain ranges,

00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:20.820
and things of this nature.

00:02:20.820 --> 00:02:23.890
So really useful to be
able to contextualize

00:02:23.890 --> 00:02:26.990
your data in maps, for sure.

00:02:26.990 --> 00:02:28.855
Now, if you have just
a small set of data,

00:02:28.855 --> 00:02:30.980
you can imagine almost
doing this analysis by hand.

00:02:30.980 --> 00:02:32.480
If you had just a few hundred
customers, and they're

00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:34.688
all coming from a certain
neighborhood-- easy enough.

00:02:34.688 --> 00:02:38.540
But in our age of information
and the accessibility

00:02:38.540 --> 00:02:41.540
we have to just beaucoup
data from all the open data

00:02:41.540 --> 00:02:46.550
sets, from weather to census
data and other sources,

00:02:46.550 --> 00:02:50.260
you can really go pretty wild
from an analytics perspective

00:02:50.260 --> 00:02:53.060
thinking about all the fun
things you can do with space.

00:02:53.060 --> 00:02:55.844
And for that, you might
be thinking about,

00:02:55.844 --> 00:02:57.260
if you had millions
of customers--

00:02:57.260 --> 00:02:59.180
let's just hypothetically say--

00:02:59.180 --> 00:03:01.670
you wanted to join that
with some of those bigger,

00:03:01.670 --> 00:03:04.220
bigger data sets, you might need
a little bit more horsepower

00:03:04.220 --> 00:03:06.830
to actually conduct
that GIS analysis.

00:03:06.830 --> 00:03:09.950
And for this, we're incredibly
excited to have launched

00:03:09.950 --> 00:03:14.700
BigQuery GIS into beta a few
weeks back, here, in September.

00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:17.127
And really what this means
for analysts, especially

00:03:17.127 --> 00:03:18.710
those that are already
using BigQuery,

00:03:18.710 --> 00:03:21.500
is that, hey, we're bringing
the GIS functionality right

00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:23.190
directly into the
data warehouse.

00:03:23.190 --> 00:03:25.580
You no longer would need to
export your data elsewhere

00:03:25.580 --> 00:03:27.900
and bringing it to a
purpose-built GIS application,

00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:28.400
right?

00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:30.240
All that core functionality
is right there

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:32.900
on top of your data and
accessible right there.

00:03:32.900 --> 00:03:35.450
And what's really unique and
different about this service

00:03:35.450 --> 00:03:38.720
is combining this
Cloud GIS functionality

00:03:38.720 --> 00:03:42.590
with the raw power of BigQuery,
and its Massively Parallel

00:03:42.590 --> 00:03:45.980
Processing, or MPP
architecture, to do big data--

00:03:45.980 --> 00:03:48.280
to kind of merge big data
and GIS in the cloud.

00:03:48.280 --> 00:03:49.455
And that's super exciting.

00:03:49.455 --> 00:03:51.330
We're really excited to
bring that to market.

00:03:51.330 --> 00:03:53.457
And Chad, being product
manager for BigQuery,

00:03:53.457 --> 00:03:55.790
is going to dive into a little
bit more details on this.

00:03:55.790 --> 00:03:56.623
CHAD JENNINGS: Yeah.

00:03:56.623 --> 00:03:58.530
And I just wanted
to say, though,

00:03:58.530 --> 00:04:00.710
that intersection
of big data and GIS

00:04:00.710 --> 00:04:03.140
is a thing that we're
really excited to address

00:04:03.140 --> 00:04:05.150
in the marketplace.

00:04:05.150 --> 00:04:06.770
So with the launch
of this, BigQuery

00:04:06.770 --> 00:04:11.120
is the only cloud MPP
enterprise data warehouse

00:04:11.120 --> 00:04:13.670
to support GIS data
types and functions

00:04:13.670 --> 00:04:14.904
as first-class citizens.

00:04:14.904 --> 00:04:16.320
And so we're really
proud of that.

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:18.829
And the engineering team has
worked on this, actually,

00:04:18.829 --> 00:04:21.420
in conjunction with
the Earth Engine team.

00:04:21.420 --> 00:04:24.320
So we used the same
computational libraries

00:04:24.320 --> 00:04:26.696
under the covers that power
things like Google Maps,

00:04:26.696 --> 00:04:27.820
Earth Engine, Google Earth.

00:04:27.820 --> 00:04:31.220
So it's really bringing a lot
of really awesome Google assets

00:04:31.220 --> 00:04:32.925
to our customers, which is fun.

00:04:32.925 --> 00:04:34.050
SOLEIL KELLEY: [INAUDIBLE].

00:04:34.050 --> 00:04:34.370
CHAD JENNINGS: All right.

00:04:34.370 --> 00:04:37.010
But first off, you may
have a question of, what

00:04:37.010 --> 00:04:39.140
is this BigQuery thing anyway?

00:04:39.140 --> 00:04:39.890
Let's start there.

00:04:39.890 --> 00:04:40.780
SOLEIL KELLEY: Sure.

00:04:40.780 --> 00:04:43.280
CHAD JENNINGS: So BigQuery is
Google Cloud's enterprise data

00:04:43.280 --> 00:04:44.150
warehouse.

00:04:44.150 --> 00:04:47.330
So you'd interact
with it in SQL.

00:04:47.330 --> 00:04:50.000
And it is serverless
and fully managed.

00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:51.740
And what that
means is, you don't

00:04:51.740 --> 00:04:55.370
have to mess with spinning up
nodes or spinning up clusters.

00:04:55.370 --> 00:04:57.380
We handle all of that for you.

00:04:57.380 --> 00:04:59.720
And so what that means
is, you bring your data,

00:04:59.720 --> 00:05:02.150
you bring your workloads,
you load them both,

00:05:02.150 --> 00:05:04.180
you press the button
that says Run Query,

00:05:04.180 --> 00:05:07.000
and we spin up all the
compute resources and storage

00:05:07.000 --> 00:05:08.420
resources that you need.

00:05:08.420 --> 00:05:11.450
That's what fully
managed means to us.

00:05:11.450 --> 00:05:15.580
This product scans-- goes
super big and super fast.

00:05:15.580 --> 00:05:18.490
Our largest customers
have hundreds of petabytes

00:05:18.490 --> 00:05:19.840
of storage with us.

00:05:19.840 --> 00:05:22.900
And our largest
queries regularly

00:05:22.900 --> 00:05:26.620
exceed 20 or almost
30 billion rows.

00:05:26.620 --> 00:05:28.300
So big data and GIS
together, right?

00:05:28.300 --> 00:05:30.010
SOLEIL KELLEY: There's that
intangible data analytics

00:05:30.010 --> 00:05:32.040
thing in the computer
box thing again, right?

00:05:32.040 --> 00:05:32.196
CHAD JENNINGS: Yeah, really.

00:05:32.196 --> 00:05:33.790
What does 30 trillion
actually mean?

00:05:33.790 --> 00:05:34.510
SOLEIL KELLEY: I have no idea.

00:05:34.510 --> 00:05:35.205
Just a lot.

00:05:35.205 --> 00:05:37.330
CHAD JENNINGS: So here's
what BigQuery GIS actually

00:05:37.330 --> 00:05:37.871
means, right?

00:05:37.871 --> 00:05:41.500
We're supporting geographic data
types, geographic functions.

00:05:41.500 --> 00:05:44.260
And we're going to go through
these in some detail in just

00:05:44.260 --> 00:05:45.460
a second, here.

00:05:45.460 --> 00:05:48.010
And then, kind of where
the payoff happens

00:05:48.010 --> 00:05:52.720
is, we have launched something
called BigQuery Geo Viz, which

00:05:52.720 --> 00:05:54.340
is a lightweight
visualization tool

00:05:54.340 --> 00:05:57.820
to put all of those cool data
that you just figured out

00:05:57.820 --> 00:05:59.720
onto a map.

00:05:59.720 --> 00:06:00.220
All right.

00:06:00.220 --> 00:06:03.300
So a little context
setting-- why bother?

00:06:03.300 --> 00:06:05.260
Euclid would look
at this map and go,

00:06:05.260 --> 00:06:06.940
that's perfectly fine, right?

00:06:06.940 --> 00:06:09.700
That is a straight line between
two points, shortest distance,

00:06:09.700 --> 00:06:10.900
that's how you should go.

00:06:10.900 --> 00:06:13.160
Except, as we all know now--

00:06:13.160 --> 00:06:14.860
Euclid didn't know
this, but we do--

00:06:14.860 --> 00:06:17.770
curved Earth-- the shortest
distance between these

00:06:17.770 --> 00:06:19.090
particular two points--

00:06:19.090 --> 00:06:22.600
Seattle and Stuttgart,
if anyone is curious--

00:06:22.600 --> 00:06:24.850
is a great circle route.

00:06:24.850 --> 00:06:27.790
And to be honest, even though
I come from a navigation

00:06:27.790 --> 00:06:31.540
background, I never
really quite got

00:06:31.540 --> 00:06:33.640
what a straight
circle route truly

00:06:33.640 --> 00:06:35.890
meant until I looked
at it visualized

00:06:35.890 --> 00:06:37.880
on Google Earth from space.

00:06:37.880 --> 00:06:39.790
So Euclid was right.

00:06:39.790 --> 00:06:41.290
A straight line
between two points--

00:06:41.290 --> 00:06:43.706
that is the right way to go
if you want to get there fast.

00:06:43.706 --> 00:06:47.060
And that is what a straight line
looks like on the curved space.

00:06:47.060 --> 00:06:50.360
So with geographic data
types and functions,

00:06:50.360 --> 00:06:52.660
we want to honor the curvature
of the earth, right--

00:06:52.660 --> 00:06:54.670
seems like a big
thing to honor--

00:06:54.670 --> 00:06:57.670
and actually do these
calculations exactly right.

00:06:57.670 --> 00:07:03.140
So these are the data types
that we're supporting--

00:07:03.140 --> 00:07:06.020
point, linestring, polygon,
all the way down to collection.

00:07:06.020 --> 00:07:07.747
So it's quite a rich dataset.

00:07:07.747 --> 00:07:09.580
All right, and now,
Soleil will take us away

00:07:09.580 --> 00:07:10.420
with the functions.

00:07:10.420 --> 00:07:11.670
SOLEIL KELLEY: Yeah, for sure.

00:07:11.670 --> 00:07:15.010
So we love our SQL
verbs, and we've just

00:07:15.010 --> 00:07:18.720
brought in about 40 new
verbs into the BigQuery

00:07:18.720 --> 00:07:20.170
as first-class citizens again.

00:07:20.170 --> 00:07:25.060
And they conform to the post-GIS
project spatial type function

00:07:25.060 --> 00:07:26.900
convention, the ST_.

00:07:26.900 --> 00:07:28.900
And we have a number of
different functions here

00:07:28.900 --> 00:07:30.820
you can see in the
table on the right.

00:07:30.820 --> 00:07:32.637
If you are familiar
with PostGIS,

00:07:32.637 --> 00:07:33.970
this will be a walk in the park.

00:07:33.970 --> 00:07:36.694
And you can maybe grab
a super quick glass

00:07:36.694 --> 00:07:37.610
of water or something.

00:07:37.610 --> 00:07:39.910
But if you're just getting
started, and want to just know,

00:07:39.910 --> 00:07:42.035
at a high level, what these
functions are all about

00:07:42.035 --> 00:07:45.640
and the things that you can
do on your geospatial data,

00:07:45.640 --> 00:07:47.800
we're just going to dive
into that super quick.

00:07:47.800 --> 00:07:49.550
Constructors-- as
the name suggests,

00:07:49.550 --> 00:07:52.210
these are really about
building new geographies

00:07:52.210 --> 00:07:53.830
from existing
coordinates of, say,

00:07:53.830 --> 00:07:56.170
a lat-long pair, or
existing geographies,

00:07:56.170 --> 00:07:59.720
like a couple of polygons or
lines, and making a collection.

00:07:59.720 --> 00:08:01.960
So the diagram here
demonstrates a set

00:08:01.960 --> 00:08:04.690
of five different lat-long
pairs and making a line out

00:08:04.690 --> 00:08:06.324
of those, right?

00:08:06.324 --> 00:08:08.240
Parsers and formatters--
I mean, obviously, we

00:08:08.240 --> 00:08:10.480
want some interoperability
between different formats.

00:08:10.480 --> 00:08:12.970
And so these are all about
creating or exporting

00:08:12.970 --> 00:08:14.560
geographies into
different formats.

00:08:14.560 --> 00:08:17.975
So from binary to a
polygon, from GeoJSON

00:08:17.975 --> 00:08:20.810
to text and things like this.

00:08:20.810 --> 00:08:23.290
These are the functions that
you would use to do that

00:08:23.290 --> 00:08:25.040
so that you have a
little bit more interop

00:08:25.040 --> 00:08:27.800
with other programs.

00:08:27.800 --> 00:08:29.420
Transformations--
again, so these

00:08:29.420 --> 00:08:33.900
are creating new geographies
similar to constructors.

00:08:33.900 --> 00:08:35.720
But they're having
similar properties

00:08:35.720 --> 00:08:37.440
as their input geographies.

00:08:37.440 --> 00:08:40.159
So here, in the diagram,
we've highlighted

00:08:40.159 --> 00:08:41.090
the centroid function.

00:08:41.090 --> 00:08:44.179
So if you wanted to find the
center of some sort of zip code

00:08:44.179 --> 00:08:46.640
polygon, you'd use
that function to create

00:08:46.640 --> 00:08:51.184
a point, a lat-long set, out
of that existing geography

00:08:51.184 --> 00:08:52.850
and many other types
of transformations,

00:08:52.850 --> 00:08:54.200
naturally, as well.

00:08:54.200 --> 00:08:56.710
Predicates-- so,
great for filtering.

00:08:56.710 --> 00:08:59.990
Is this region, or the
data within this region,

00:08:59.990 --> 00:09:03.046
existing within another region,
in this particular zip code,

00:09:03.046 --> 00:09:05.420
or something-- you know,
yes/no, or true/false questions,

00:09:05.420 --> 00:09:06.380
rather--

00:09:06.380 --> 00:09:11.162
so great for filtering your
geographic data and whatnot.

00:09:11.162 --> 00:09:12.620
Accessories-- so
sometimes you just

00:09:12.620 --> 00:09:16.310
need to know a little bit of the
metadata about your geography

00:09:16.310 --> 00:09:17.090
data.

00:09:17.090 --> 00:09:20.660
And so for this, we have a
number of functions here for,

00:09:20.660 --> 00:09:22.970
like, how many vertices or
how many points are there

00:09:22.970 --> 00:09:24.000
as part of this polygon?

00:09:24.000 --> 00:09:25.800
So you can ask those
types of questions.

00:09:25.800 --> 00:09:26.390
Is it a point?

00:09:26.390 --> 00:09:26.840
Is it a line?

00:09:26.840 --> 00:09:27.390
Is a Polygon?

00:09:27.390 --> 00:09:29.706
If you just get a whole
bunch of geographic data,

00:09:29.706 --> 00:09:31.580
you could ask those
types of questions there.

00:09:31.580 --> 00:09:33.729
Measures-- as the name
suggests, pretty intuitive.

00:09:33.729 --> 00:09:34.770
But what's the perimeter?

00:09:34.770 --> 00:09:38.120
What's the area, distances
between points, et cetera?

00:09:38.120 --> 00:09:39.800
These are real
core functionality.

00:09:39.800 --> 00:09:42.256
CHAD JENNINGS: Right--
not flashy, but important.

00:09:42.256 --> 00:09:43.130
And here's the flash.

00:09:43.130 --> 00:09:44.390
SOLEIL KELLEY: Super,
super important--

00:09:44.390 --> 00:09:46.265
I mean, these are, like,
when you immediately

00:09:46.265 --> 00:09:48.080
think about GIS data,
at least for me,

00:09:48.080 --> 00:09:49.280
you're asking questions,
like, wait, what's

00:09:49.280 --> 00:09:50.700
the distance between x and y?

00:09:50.700 --> 00:09:52.325
And these are questions
you often have.

00:09:52.325 --> 00:09:53.660
But yeah, joins are [INAUDIBLE].

00:09:53.660 --> 00:09:56.240
CHAD JENNINGS: So this is
where the real magic starts.

00:09:56.240 --> 00:09:59.660
And doing joins on geographic
datasets-- in the demos

00:09:59.660 --> 00:10:02.430
we'll talk about it in a second,
we actually join on zip code.

00:10:02.430 --> 00:10:05.030
But we're actually joining
on the zip code integer.

00:10:05.030 --> 00:10:07.700
With these functions, you
can actually-- or sorry--

00:10:07.700 --> 00:10:10.700
the integer that measure or
that identifies the zip code.

00:10:10.700 --> 00:10:13.250
With these, you can actually
do joins on the geography,

00:10:13.250 --> 00:10:16.380
like, find all the points that
are in these two datasets.

00:10:16.380 --> 00:10:18.380
Join them together with
any of these predicates.

00:10:18.380 --> 00:10:22.704
So this is where the magic
really happens, here.

00:10:22.704 --> 00:10:24.520
SOLEIL KELLEY: Cool.

00:10:24.520 --> 00:10:25.820
CHAD JENNINGS: OK.

00:10:25.820 --> 00:10:29.490
But in terms of, like, eye
candy, this is the magic.

00:10:29.490 --> 00:10:30.950
So this is BigQuery Geo Viz.

00:10:30.950 --> 00:10:32.510
And you can see
from the GIF here

00:10:32.510 --> 00:10:36.050
that you can compose
a query, run a query,

00:10:36.050 --> 00:10:39.140
and then style the results
in a map, all interactively.

00:10:39.140 --> 00:10:42.260
It's a lightweight tool, so this
isn't going to handle millions

00:10:42.260 --> 00:10:43.550
upon millions--

00:10:43.550 --> 00:10:46.730
it's limited to
about 2,000 points.

00:10:46.730 --> 00:10:48.230
But it solves the use case of--

00:10:48.230 --> 00:10:51.980
I'm an analyst, I wrote a query,
please let me see that on a map

00:10:51.980 --> 00:10:55.400
just to make sure that I'm
sane or that I got the results

00:10:55.400 --> 00:10:56.590
that I expected.

00:10:56.590 --> 00:10:58.120
SOLEIL KELLEY: Great for
ad-hoc exploration, yeah.

00:10:58.120 --> 00:10:59.078
CHAD JENNINGS: Exactly.

00:10:59.078 --> 00:11:01.670
And if you've got more
serious mapping needs,

00:11:01.670 --> 00:11:04.550
you can export a table
from BigQuery into GCS,

00:11:04.550 --> 00:11:06.779
and then import that
into Earth Engine.

00:11:06.779 --> 00:11:08.195
And then here, you
use JavaScript,

00:11:08.195 --> 00:11:12.020
and you can create maps of
arbitrary complexity and

00:11:12.020 --> 00:11:13.460
arbitrary beautifulness.

00:11:13.460 --> 00:11:14.420
Is that a word?

00:11:14.420 --> 00:11:16.128
SOLEIL KELLEY: It
should be, if it's not.

00:11:16.128 --> 00:11:18.295
CHAD JENNINGS: There it is.

00:11:18.295 --> 00:11:22.460
Okey-doke, so we'll dive
into a couple of demos here.

00:11:22.460 --> 00:11:26.330
And so referencing the example
that Soleil talked about

00:11:26.330 --> 00:11:31.070
earlier, we're going to
pretend that we are retail site

00:11:31.070 --> 00:11:32.000
selectors.

00:11:32.000 --> 00:11:33.560
And so we have a store.

00:11:33.560 --> 00:11:37.820
And Soleil, the target
demographic of your store is--

00:11:37.820 --> 00:11:39.620
SOLEIL KELLEY: 25 to 34.

00:11:39.620 --> 00:11:42.294
CHAD JENNINGS: How about
25 to 44, since that's--

00:11:42.294 --> 00:11:43.460
since that's what I prepped?

00:11:43.460 --> 00:11:44.480
SOLEIL KELLEY: Yeah,
let's do that one.

00:11:44.480 --> 00:11:45.855
CHAD JENNINGS:
Let's do that one.

00:11:45.855 --> 00:11:46.610
OK.

00:11:46.610 --> 00:11:47.870
All right.

00:11:47.870 --> 00:11:53.770
So here, let's cut
over to the demo.

00:11:53.770 --> 00:11:55.716
SOLEIL KELLEY: What
are we looking at here?

00:11:55.716 --> 00:11:59.400
CHAD JENNINGS: So this
is the BigQuery web UI.

00:11:59.400 --> 00:12:03.530
And what we see here is,
on the left panel here,

00:12:03.530 --> 00:12:07.482
there are a bunch of, basically,
asset navigators, right?

00:12:07.482 --> 00:12:08.690
You can look at your queries.

00:12:08.690 --> 00:12:10.640
You can look at saved
queries, the job history

00:12:10.640 --> 00:12:12.200
that you've run in this project.

00:12:12.200 --> 00:12:14.270
You can even look at datasets.

00:12:14.270 --> 00:12:17.150
Down here, I'll double click
into the BigQuery public data.

00:12:17.150 --> 00:12:19.092
And there's now a
whole bunch of stuff.

00:12:19.092 --> 00:12:20.550
The baseball dataset
is pretty fun.

00:12:20.550 --> 00:12:22.710
We're not going to do
that one today, sorry.

00:12:22.710 --> 00:12:25.430
SOLEIL KELLEY: Unless my
customers are baseball folks.

00:12:25.430 --> 00:12:25.710
CHAD JENNINGS: Right.

00:12:25.710 --> 00:12:26.780
Right now it's a retail shop.

00:12:26.780 --> 00:12:26.840
SOLEIL KELLEY: OK.

00:12:26.840 --> 00:12:29.360
CHAD JENNINGS: Any case,
the left panel here,

00:12:29.360 --> 00:12:31.280
to navigate assets--

00:12:31.280 --> 00:12:33.960
this window right here's
the query composer window.

00:12:33.960 --> 00:12:36.080
So this is where
you write your SQL.

00:12:36.080 --> 00:12:38.240
And then you get your
results back down here

00:12:38.240 --> 00:12:39.104
in the lower pane.

00:12:39.104 --> 00:12:40.520
And so what I'm
going to do is I'm

00:12:40.520 --> 00:12:43.130
going to walk you through
this query real quick.

00:12:43.130 --> 00:12:45.890
So one thing that I
like to do a lot in SQL

00:12:45.890 --> 00:12:48.170
is use these WITH statements
to pull parameters up

00:12:48.170 --> 00:12:49.250
to the top of a query.

00:12:49.250 --> 00:12:50.916
It just means that,
if you want to share

00:12:50.916 --> 00:12:54.470
that query with somebody, or if
you want to adjust a parameter,

00:12:54.470 --> 00:12:56.720
you don't have to go searching
through lines and lines

00:12:56.720 --> 00:12:58.980
and lines of SQL to get to it.

00:12:58.980 --> 00:13:00.830
So we're going to
set parameters.

00:13:00.830 --> 00:13:02.810
We're going to pick
latitude and longitude.

00:13:02.810 --> 00:13:04.393
That's the center
of Seattle, so we'll

00:13:04.393 --> 00:13:06.590
pretend we're going
to put a store there.

00:13:06.590 --> 00:13:09.440
You could get this very
simply by just googling

00:13:09.440 --> 00:13:11.630
center of Seattle or
googling an address,

00:13:11.630 --> 00:13:13.580
and it will return
you the lat-long.

00:13:13.580 --> 00:13:15.650
And then, for this
one, we're going

00:13:15.650 --> 00:13:19.940
to stipulate the
radius as 1 mile.

00:13:19.940 --> 00:13:24.110
And then, this set of code
pulls all of the zip codes

00:13:24.110 --> 00:13:25.190
within that area.

00:13:25.190 --> 00:13:27.550
So it uses this ST_DWithin.

00:13:27.550 --> 00:13:31.000
So it creates a point from
the latitude and longitude,

00:13:31.000 --> 00:13:33.130
and then makes short.

00:13:33.130 --> 00:13:37.000
And then it looks at the zip
area latitude and longitude

00:13:37.000 --> 00:13:43.930
and finds all of the zip codes
that are within 1,609 meters.

00:13:43.930 --> 00:13:45.700
SOLEIL KELLEY:
Happens to be 1 mile.

00:13:45.700 --> 00:13:48.280
CHAD JENNINGS: 1 mile,
that's right, thank you.

00:13:48.280 --> 00:13:49.960
The next set of
this code is where

00:13:49.960 --> 00:13:51.670
we're going to pull the stats.

00:13:51.670 --> 00:13:53.620
And so this table
that we're looking at

00:13:53.620 --> 00:13:58.000
is actually available in public
dataset inside of BigQuery.

00:13:58.000 --> 00:13:59.620
It's called-- no surprise--

00:13:59.620 --> 00:14:02.350
Population by ZIP 2010.

00:14:02.350 --> 00:14:06.010
And so what this code
is doing is simply

00:14:06.010 --> 00:14:08.890
adding up the population
totals from these different

00:14:08.890 --> 00:14:10.690
demographic buckets.

00:14:10.690 --> 00:14:13.630
This dataset only
has age and gender.

00:14:13.630 --> 00:14:17.290
If you look at the US Census
page or the American Community

00:14:17.290 --> 00:14:20.140
Survey-- the Fact Finder page
is really useful for this--

00:14:20.140 --> 00:14:23.710
they have many, many, many
more demographic buckets.

00:14:23.710 --> 00:14:25.720
But we'll focus on these.

00:14:25.720 --> 00:14:27.790
And then, at the
end, we're just going

00:14:27.790 --> 00:14:31.300
to pick all of those zip
codes, the zip code stats,

00:14:31.300 --> 00:14:34.120
and the zip code geometries.

00:14:34.120 --> 00:14:36.790
And we're going to pull them
out into a single table.

00:14:36.790 --> 00:14:39.320
And so I've run the query.

00:14:39.320 --> 00:14:40.750
And it was cached.

00:14:40.750 --> 00:14:43.247
The 0.017 is-- it
used the cache.

00:14:43.247 --> 00:14:44.830
I prepped this ahead
of time because I

00:14:44.830 --> 00:14:48.880
didn't want to burden you
all with watching query run.

00:14:48.880 --> 00:14:50.660
But what you can see
here is this table.

00:14:50.660 --> 00:14:53.524
So here's 98154.

00:14:53.524 --> 00:14:54.940
This is actually
a tiny little zip

00:14:54.940 --> 00:14:58.030
code that's just for the
purposes of the US Post Office,

00:14:58.030 --> 00:14:59.860
so no people live in it.

00:14:59.860 --> 00:15:02.560
But you can see these
are the populations,

00:15:02.560 --> 00:15:04.120
and then here's the polygon.

00:15:04.120 --> 00:15:06.790
And that polygon
string is totally

00:15:06.790 --> 00:15:08.380
parsable by human readers.

00:15:08.380 --> 00:15:12.549
And you look at that, and
you're, like, oh, -122.333564.

00:15:12.549 --> 00:15:14.090
Yeah, that's downtown
Seattle, right?

00:15:14.090 --> 00:15:14.745
SOLEIL KELLEY: I can see it.

00:15:14.745 --> 00:15:15.786
CHAD JENNINGS: Right, no.

00:15:15.786 --> 00:15:16.960
Nobody does that.

00:15:16.960 --> 00:15:20.420
So what we really want to do is,
we want to see that on a map.

00:15:20.420 --> 00:15:23.300
So let's walk through how
BigQuery Geo Viz works.

00:15:23.300 --> 00:15:25.620
So I've actually prepopulated
this one as well.

00:15:25.620 --> 00:15:27.550
And you can see
that I've increased

00:15:27.550 --> 00:15:30.550
the radius to 15 miles.

00:15:30.550 --> 00:15:31.750
It's exactly the same query.

00:15:31.750 --> 00:15:34.750
So I have copied from
the composer window

00:15:34.750 --> 00:15:39.100
and pasted into the
BigQuery Geo Viz window

00:15:39.100 --> 00:15:41.200
and run the results to
get this map over here.

00:15:41.200 --> 00:15:43.960
And what's cool about
this tool is that you

00:15:43.960 --> 00:15:45.970
can style interactively.

00:15:45.970 --> 00:15:49.000
So the fill color
for this choropleth,

00:15:49.000 --> 00:15:53.830
I have chosen to be
population of 65-plus.

00:15:53.830 --> 00:15:56.300
But if I wanted to change
that, I could, in real time.

00:15:56.300 --> 00:15:57.966
As a matter of fact,
we're going to dive

00:15:57.966 --> 00:16:00.867
in to the north end
of Seattle here.

00:16:00.867 --> 00:16:01.450
You know what?

00:16:01.450 --> 00:16:03.310
That's a little bit
opaque for my taste.

00:16:03.310 --> 00:16:05.145
I'm going to lighten
the fill opacity.

00:16:05.145 --> 00:16:06.854
I'm going make it 0.5.

00:16:06.854 --> 00:16:08.520
There, that lightens
it up a little bit.

00:16:08.520 --> 00:16:09.820
It's a little easier to see.

00:16:09.820 --> 00:16:11.960
Go back to Fill Color.

00:16:11.960 --> 00:16:14.170
And we're going to
look at a couple

00:16:14.170 --> 00:16:16.600
of different demographics--
so demographic number one.

00:16:16.600 --> 00:16:17.810
SOLEIL KELLEY:
There's my 25 to 44.

00:16:17.810 --> 00:16:18.080
Thank you.

00:16:18.080 --> 00:16:19.080
CHAD JENNINGS: Right on.

00:16:19.080 --> 00:16:21.397
So let's see where your
target demographic is.

00:16:21.397 --> 00:16:23.480
We'll change the range a
little bit, since their--

00:16:23.480 --> 00:16:24.313
SOLEIL KELLEY: Yeah.

00:16:24.313 --> 00:16:26.225
The max is 21,000.

00:16:26.225 --> 00:16:27.550
There you go.

00:16:27.550 --> 00:16:29.380
CHAD JENNINGS: And
so what you see is,

00:16:29.380 --> 00:16:33.970
if we zero in on this
zip code-- so 98103--

00:16:33.970 --> 00:16:35.930
there's a concentration here.

00:16:35.930 --> 00:16:39.820
And there is a dearth of your
target demographic in 98199.

00:16:39.820 --> 00:16:42.850
So don't put your
store out there.

00:16:42.850 --> 00:16:44.010
SOLEIL KELLEY: No.

00:16:44.010 --> 00:16:45.460
CHAD JENNINGS: But what I
wanted to point out here

00:16:45.460 --> 00:16:47.560
was, let's go ahead and
have a look at-- well,

00:16:47.560 --> 00:16:48.490
let's expand it.

00:16:48.490 --> 00:16:51.620
Let's say you were looking
for college-age students.

00:16:51.620 --> 00:16:55.440
And then, this zip
code here lights up.

00:16:55.440 --> 00:16:56.520
What's there?

00:16:56.520 --> 00:16:57.790
SOLEIL KELLEY: There happens
to be a university there.

00:16:57.790 --> 00:16:58.180
CHAD JENNINGS: Right.

00:16:58.180 --> 00:16:59.350
SOLEIL KELLEY: And we haven't
even changed the range,

00:16:59.350 --> 00:17:01.909
but you can see it's actually
a similar range set, there.

00:17:01.909 --> 00:17:02.950
CHAD JENNINGS: Oh, right.

00:17:02.950 --> 00:17:03.650
Yeah, I can do that.

00:17:03.650 --> 00:17:05.483
SOLEIL KELLEY: But a
very high concentration

00:17:05.483 --> 00:17:07.089
there of college-age students.

00:17:07.089 --> 00:17:09.130
CHAD JENNINGS: So no
surprise, the University

00:17:09.130 --> 00:17:10.569
of Washington lights up.

00:17:10.569 --> 00:17:14.500
And then, if we
look at the 65-plus,

00:17:14.500 --> 00:17:20.060
and I adjust the
range here to 7,000,

00:17:20.060 --> 00:17:22.280
you can see that the
population is just

00:17:22.280 --> 00:17:25.040
starting to move, not just
north, but out, right?

00:17:25.040 --> 00:17:28.089
So this is flight from the
urban center, I suppose.

00:17:28.089 --> 00:17:32.450
And folks in this
demographic are moving away

00:17:32.450 --> 00:17:34.060
from the city center.

00:17:34.060 --> 00:17:34.640
OK.

00:17:34.640 --> 00:17:37.730
So what we've shown
here is the ability

00:17:37.730 --> 00:17:41.090
to do geospatial analysis
and then style a map

00:17:41.090 --> 00:17:42.830
and visualize a
map in real time.

00:17:42.830 --> 00:17:44.460
If you wanted to
share this with folks,

00:17:44.460 --> 00:17:46.585
you can just take a screen
shot or share the query.

00:17:46.585 --> 00:17:49.090
SOLEIL KELLEY: You can even
make that nice and big.

00:17:49.090 --> 00:17:49.690
CHAD JENNINGS: Oh, yeah.

00:17:49.690 --> 00:17:50.190
Here.

00:17:52.960 --> 00:17:53.750
So I'm sorry.

00:17:53.750 --> 00:17:58.960
We can zoom out and
see the entire extent.

00:17:58.960 --> 00:18:00.700
Okey-doke.

00:18:00.700 --> 00:18:04.000
So geospatial
information is useful

00:18:04.000 --> 00:18:08.420
partly because it's not focused
in any one particular area.

00:18:08.420 --> 00:18:13.780
So I ran this query
again for New York.

00:18:13.780 --> 00:18:18.220
And so again, this is
the 0 to 24 demographic.

00:18:18.220 --> 00:18:21.730
And if we just click over
to this other tab here,

00:18:21.730 --> 00:18:24.190
you can see that--

00:18:24.190 --> 00:18:25.360
sorry.

00:18:25.360 --> 00:18:29.080
This one-- let's
see, at the styling.

00:18:29.080 --> 00:18:31.330
This one is the 65-plus.

00:18:31.330 --> 00:18:33.730
I'll switch back and
forth between these tabs.

00:18:33.730 --> 00:18:40.390
You can see that these
neighborhoods out here,

00:18:40.390 --> 00:18:43.150
in the south of New York,
get quite a bit darker.

00:18:43.150 --> 00:18:47.620
So what that means is folks are
kind of moving out to the beach

00:18:47.620 --> 00:18:51.280
as they get tired
of the city life.

00:18:51.280 --> 00:18:52.270
OK.

00:18:52.270 --> 00:18:53.530
So that's interesting.

00:18:53.530 --> 00:18:55.900
So now we've given these
retail site selectors

00:18:55.900 --> 00:18:59.230
the tools to go ahead and
look at different areas

00:18:59.230 --> 00:19:00.760
and see what the
demographics are.

00:19:00.760 --> 00:19:04.060
To be honest, zip codes are
pretty coarse-grained geometry

00:19:04.060 --> 00:19:04.900
for this analysis.

00:19:04.900 --> 00:19:07.510
You'd rather use census
tracts or census blocks.

00:19:07.510 --> 00:19:10.030
Again, you can get those from
the American Community Survey

00:19:10.030 --> 00:19:11.380
page.

00:19:11.380 --> 00:19:12.580
Go to the Download tab.

00:19:12.580 --> 00:19:15.370
That's where you can
download census tracts or zip

00:19:15.370 --> 00:19:17.290
codes for the
entire United States

00:19:17.290 --> 00:19:20.590
and bring that into BigQuery.

00:19:20.590 --> 00:19:22.390
We're going to look
at this last query

00:19:22.390 --> 00:19:24.460
here because this kind
of the summary table.

00:19:24.460 --> 00:19:30.440
So same kind of construction--
so with stats by zip code.

00:19:30.440 --> 00:19:33.080
This is actually the
same code as before.

00:19:33.080 --> 00:19:34.660
And what we're
going to do here is

00:19:34.660 --> 00:19:39.962
we're going to run those stats
for a collection of radii.

00:19:39.962 --> 00:19:41.670
So we're going to
create a summary table,

00:19:41.670 --> 00:19:46.397
like, show me the list
of people that live 1,

00:19:46.397 --> 00:19:52.610
10, 20, 50 miles from
my chosen location.

00:19:52.610 --> 00:19:55.870
And again, we're using
the BigQuery GIS functions

00:19:55.870 --> 00:19:58.180
to construct that filter.

00:19:58.180 --> 00:20:00.760
All right, and then here's
the resulting table.

00:20:00.760 --> 00:20:04.390
And now, this is not GIS,
but it is super convenient.

00:20:04.390 --> 00:20:08.020
You look at this button here
called Exploring Data Studio,

00:20:08.020 --> 00:20:09.940
you can actually click
on that, and that

00:20:09.940 --> 00:20:13.910
will materialize the results
in a Data Studio session.

00:20:13.910 --> 00:20:16.030
Let's go ahead-- so let's see--

00:20:16.030 --> 00:20:18.970
the dimension we're
going to use is r.

00:20:18.970 --> 00:20:19.870
So that's the Radius.

00:20:19.870 --> 00:20:21.490
We'll get rid of that one.

00:20:21.490 --> 00:20:24.520
And then we'll do population.

00:20:24.520 --> 00:20:26.020
SOLEIL KELLEY: While
Chad is pulling

00:20:26.020 --> 00:20:27.978
in these different
demographic groups, as well,

00:20:27.978 --> 00:20:30.560
[INAUDIBLE] is something we
just launched into GA last week,

00:20:30.560 --> 00:20:31.270
which is super exciting.

00:20:31.270 --> 00:20:32.950
And this particular
functionality-- that

00:20:32.950 --> 00:20:36.170
integration between BigQuery and
Data Studio, that one-click UI

00:20:36.170 --> 00:20:38.670
experience is something that
we launched earlier this summer

00:20:38.670 --> 00:20:40.630
at our Cloud Next event.

00:20:40.630 --> 00:20:43.630
And it's been a very
popular feature.

00:20:43.630 --> 00:20:45.460
Our customers have
been asking about it.

00:20:45.460 --> 00:20:46.780
It was really nice to
be able to deliver that.

00:20:46.780 --> 00:20:48.820
And people have been
responding well to that.

00:20:48.820 --> 00:20:49.420
It's been fun.

00:20:49.420 --> 00:20:49.840
CHAD JENNINGS: Excellent.

00:20:49.840 --> 00:20:51.756
SOLEIL KELLEY: And it's
just super quick for--

00:20:51.756 --> 00:20:55.090
just like we had the
BigQuery Geo Viz application

00:20:55.090 --> 00:20:57.250
to be able to quickly
explore your geospatial data,

00:20:57.250 --> 00:20:59.590
you can do the same thing
here with summary tables,

00:20:59.590 --> 00:21:01.924
with other data, to quickly
visualize it in Data Studio.

00:21:01.924 --> 00:21:02.756
CHAD JENNINGS: Yeah.

00:21:02.756 --> 00:21:04.690
And so with just a few
clicks and a little bit

00:21:04.690 --> 00:21:07.870
of clumsy dragging of
these little tickets,

00:21:07.870 --> 00:21:09.590
you can create a chart.

00:21:09.590 --> 00:21:11.920
You can then save it,
copy it to report,

00:21:11.920 --> 00:21:14.140
and share it with folks,
and they can interact

00:21:14.140 --> 00:21:16.190
with your query as well.

00:21:16.190 --> 00:21:19.510
So anyway, we wanted
to get that one out.

00:21:19.510 --> 00:21:22.840
We've got one more demo to
talk about, which is actually

00:21:22.840 --> 00:21:24.610
a totally different persona.

00:21:24.610 --> 00:21:27.447
So now we're done with
being real estate moguls.

00:21:27.447 --> 00:21:28.780
SOLEIL KELLEY: Or retail moguls.

00:21:28.780 --> 00:21:30.820
CHAD JENNINGS: Or retail moguls.

00:21:30.820 --> 00:21:34.010
Now, were city
planners from Chicago.

00:21:34.010 --> 00:21:36.100
And so our customer
Geotab actually

00:21:36.100 --> 00:21:37.570
built this application.

00:21:37.570 --> 00:21:40.090
And so what you're
seeing here is a map

00:21:40.090 --> 00:21:42.327
of hazardous driving behavior.

00:21:42.327 --> 00:21:44.410
And you might naturally
ask yourself the question,

00:21:44.410 --> 00:21:45.868
well, how does
Geotab know anything

00:21:45.868 --> 00:21:47.440
about hazardous
driving application?

00:21:47.440 --> 00:21:48.470
Great question.

00:21:48.470 --> 00:21:50.050
Thanks for asking.

00:21:50.050 --> 00:21:52.150
Geotab is an asset
tracking company

00:21:52.150 --> 00:21:53.590
and a telematics company.

00:21:53.590 --> 00:21:57.280
And so, for example, UPS--

00:21:57.280 --> 00:22:00.640
all of the UPS trucks have a box
about yea big in their truck.

00:22:00.640 --> 00:22:04.630
And that box measures location,
velocity, acceleration,

00:22:04.630 --> 00:22:06.070
plus a host of other variables.

00:22:06.070 --> 00:22:06.600
SOLEIL KELLEY: Temperature--
[INAUDIBLE],, yeah.

00:22:06.600 --> 00:22:07.558
CHAD JENNINGS: Exactly.

00:22:07.558 --> 00:22:10.270
So Geotab actually has an
incredibly rich dataset

00:22:10.270 --> 00:22:13.499
collected by 1.2
million vehicles running

00:22:13.499 --> 00:22:14.290
around the country.

00:22:14.290 --> 00:22:18.280
I think their daily intake
is about 3 billion points.

00:22:18.280 --> 00:22:20.680
All of that gets stored
inside of BigQuery,

00:22:20.680 --> 00:22:24.350
which you'll find out soon is a
very convenient place to do it.

00:22:24.350 --> 00:22:27.460
So what the map shows
here is areas in Chicago

00:22:27.460 --> 00:22:29.860
that register hazardous
driving behavior.

00:22:29.860 --> 00:22:32.050
And that's characterized
by extreme amounts

00:22:32.050 --> 00:22:36.100
of acceleration-- either
forward and back, or lateral.

00:22:36.100 --> 00:22:38.800
And what this left
panel is going to do

00:22:38.800 --> 00:22:40.270
is, we're actually
going to combine

00:22:40.270 --> 00:22:42.561
a few different technologies
here in just a few clicks.

00:22:42.561 --> 00:22:46.120
So we have BigQuery GIS,
which is going to call out

00:22:46.120 --> 00:22:47.650
the points from Chicago.

00:22:47.650 --> 00:22:50.350
We have, obviously, Google Maps.

00:22:50.350 --> 00:22:54.190
And we have BigQuery
ML, which is going to--

00:22:54.190 --> 00:22:57.160
they've actually trained a model
to predict hazardous driving

00:22:57.160 --> 00:22:59.710
behavior, i.e., those
accelerations, based

00:22:59.710 --> 00:23:01.150
on weather data.

00:23:01.150 --> 00:23:01.450
SOLEIL KELLEY: [INAUDIBLE]

00:23:01.450 --> 00:23:02.560
CHAD JENNINGS: Now,
the next question

00:23:02.560 --> 00:23:04.300
is, where did you
get the weather data?

00:23:04.300 --> 00:23:07.120
Another excellent question.

00:23:07.120 --> 00:23:09.460
He's awesome.

00:23:09.460 --> 00:23:12.132
NOAA actually hosts
weather data in BigQuery.

00:23:12.132 --> 00:23:13.090
SOLEIL KELLEY: They do.

00:23:13.090 --> 00:23:16.600
CHAD JENNINGS: And so joining
your data with weather data

00:23:16.600 --> 00:23:18.820
is literally only a
join away because it's

00:23:18.820 --> 00:23:20.810
all hosted in the
same backend storage.

00:23:20.810 --> 00:23:21.310
All right.

00:23:21.310 --> 00:23:22.935
So here, that's enough
context setting.

00:23:22.935 --> 00:23:23.690
Let's get to it.

00:23:23.690 --> 00:23:25.340
So we're going to dial up
some weather conditions.

00:23:25.340 --> 00:23:26.990
So I'm reducing the temperature.

00:23:26.990 --> 00:23:28.515
So we're going to
make it winter.

00:23:28.515 --> 00:23:30.790
I'm going to reduce
the visibility.

00:23:30.790 --> 00:23:32.620
I'm going to order a snow storm.

00:23:32.620 --> 00:23:35.270
And then, we're going to
pretend it's the holiday season,

00:23:35.270 --> 00:23:37.720
and we're going to bump
up the traffic volume.

00:23:37.720 --> 00:23:40.360
And then I just clicked
Run Predictive Analysis.

00:23:40.360 --> 00:23:43.910
And we get a map that's a lot
hotter than the original one.

00:23:43.910 --> 00:23:44.410
OK.

00:23:44.410 --> 00:23:45.130
That's interesting.

00:23:45.130 --> 00:23:45.590
SOLEIL KELLEY: Make sense?

00:23:45.590 --> 00:23:47.415
Although colder
because it's winter--

00:23:47.415 --> 00:23:49.540
it's hotter in terms of
hazardous driving behavior.

00:23:49.540 --> 00:23:52.460
CHAD JENNINGS: I
totally get you.

00:23:52.460 --> 00:23:56.380
Any case, we're going to
look in at one of these

00:23:56.380 --> 00:23:59.230
because, as the traffic planner
for the city of Chicago,

00:23:59.230 --> 00:24:03.180
we want to investigate these
points and see what we can do.

00:24:03.180 --> 00:24:05.950
And in particular, there's
one that we're going to dive

00:24:05.950 --> 00:24:10.030
into right here because--

00:24:10.030 --> 00:24:11.680
oh, here it is--

00:24:11.680 --> 00:24:13.990
because it is just down
the street from a school.

00:24:13.990 --> 00:24:16.780
So being elected
officials, we're

00:24:16.780 --> 00:24:18.760
going to prioritize
safety of constituents.

00:24:18.760 --> 00:24:21.790
We're going to prioritize safety
of vulnerable constituents,

00:24:21.790 --> 00:24:23.750
focus on hazardous
driving around schools.

00:24:23.750 --> 00:24:25.300
SOLEIL KELLEY: And the
efficiency through the network

00:24:25.300 --> 00:24:26.520
of streets for everything.

00:24:26.520 --> 00:24:27.370
CHAD JENNINGS: Yeah.

00:24:27.370 --> 00:24:28.570
We want to keep our kids safe.

00:24:28.570 --> 00:24:29.070
All right.

00:24:29.070 --> 00:24:30.200
So what's going on here?

00:24:30.200 --> 00:24:32.324
So it's interesting that
there is hazardous driving

00:24:32.324 --> 00:24:35.020
behavior in inclement weather.

00:24:35.020 --> 00:24:36.230
But what's going on?

00:24:36.230 --> 00:24:38.800
So this is where we get
a little bit of a benefit

00:24:38.800 --> 00:24:40.420
from being in the
Google ecosystem.

00:24:40.420 --> 00:24:42.430
And we're going to
drop the Street View

00:24:42.430 --> 00:24:45.760
avatar into this scene.

00:24:45.760 --> 00:24:47.530
And so we're going
to turn around.

00:24:47.530 --> 00:24:49.900
So here's the school.

00:24:49.900 --> 00:24:53.050
And then I'm going to move
just a little bit east.

00:24:53.050 --> 00:24:54.520
And look at what's here.

00:24:54.520 --> 00:24:57.190
So I'll make this screen
a bit bigger for you,

00:24:57.190 --> 00:24:59.550
and we'll zoom in just a touch.

00:24:59.550 --> 00:25:00.404
It's a bike rack.

00:25:00.404 --> 00:25:01.570
SOLEIL KELLEY: There you go.

00:25:01.570 --> 00:25:03.370
CHAD JENNINGS: Right
next to an alleyway.

00:25:03.370 --> 00:25:05.050
So agreed.

00:25:05.050 --> 00:25:07.330
I dealt up a winter day.

00:25:07.330 --> 00:25:11.260
But maybe some kids are riding
their bike down this alleyway

00:25:11.260 --> 00:25:14.140
and causing some kind of traffic
congestion or traffic issue

00:25:14.140 --> 00:25:15.400
here.

00:25:15.400 --> 00:25:18.400
Let's spin around and
see what's going on.

00:25:18.400 --> 00:25:20.320
I don't see any
traffic signage, right?

00:25:20.320 --> 00:25:22.300
So just down here, at
the end of this picture

00:25:22.300 --> 00:25:25.840
is where that hazardous
driving behavior was occurring.

00:25:25.840 --> 00:25:27.910
But there's no
traffic signage here.

00:25:27.910 --> 00:25:30.730
Maybe the remediation is
to preposition some sand.

00:25:30.730 --> 00:25:33.280
Maybe the right remediation
is to put a stop sign in here.

00:25:33.280 --> 00:25:34.630
SOLEIL KELLEY: Even
a crosswalk, yeah.

00:25:34.630 --> 00:25:36.629
CHAD JENNINGS: Or a
crosswalk, yeah, good point.

00:25:36.629 --> 00:25:40.900
But what we've enabled
here is, our city planner

00:25:40.900 --> 00:25:46.720
can now scan the entire city
using GIS, ML, BigQuery, Google

00:25:46.720 --> 00:25:49.780
Maps, Street View, public data--

00:25:49.780 --> 00:25:53.010
all without leaving their chair.

00:25:53.010 --> 00:25:55.980
And that is pretty darn cool.

00:25:55.980 --> 00:25:59.550
Here, I'll take it out
of the full screen mode.

00:25:59.550 --> 00:26:00.750
All right.

00:26:00.750 --> 00:26:04.160
Anyway, so thank you for
going on that little journey.

00:26:04.160 --> 00:26:05.674
So we did that demo.

00:26:05.674 --> 00:26:07.590
We just finished this
one, and I skipped ahead

00:26:07.590 --> 00:26:08.760
