00:28:19.860 --> 00:28:23.490
So first off, I have a few ESRI
shapefiles I'd like to use--

00:28:23.490 --> 00:28:24.990
super, super common.

00:28:24.990 --> 00:28:26.640
Can I use them
with BigQuery GIS?

00:28:26.640 --> 00:28:28.830
The answer is yes,
although you would

00:28:28.830 --> 00:28:31.980
need to convert that
shapefile format into,

00:28:31.980 --> 00:28:34.410
either well-known text or
some of the other formats

00:28:34.410 --> 00:28:35.950
that we can then
bring in, right?

00:28:35.950 --> 00:28:38.400
CHAD JENNINGS: Well, we got
this question a lot right

00:28:38.400 --> 00:28:39.980
after we launched the alpha.

00:28:39.980 --> 00:28:43.590
And so we actually have-- and
we'll put it up in a Stack

00:28:43.590 --> 00:28:45.030
Overflow topic--

00:28:45.030 --> 00:28:49.140
we actually have a document
that one of our colleagues

00:28:49.140 --> 00:28:51.835
wrote that details
exactly how to do--

00:28:51.835 --> 00:28:54.210
what's the right tool to use,
and how do you bring it in.

00:28:54.210 --> 00:28:55.847
But essentially,
Soleil is right.

00:28:55.847 --> 00:28:57.930
You have to convert the
shapefile into the formats

00:28:57.930 --> 00:28:59.430
that we support.

00:28:59.430 --> 00:29:02.886
And then you can look at them
just like we did in the demos.

00:29:02.886 --> 00:29:04.260
SOLEIL KELLEY:
Our next question.

00:29:04.260 --> 00:29:07.087
"Does BigQuery GIS have
geocoding capabilities?"

00:29:07.087 --> 00:29:07.920
CHAD JENNINGS: Yeah.

00:29:07.920 --> 00:29:11.380
So we rely on the Google
Maps APIs for this.

00:29:11.380 --> 00:29:14.580
So BigQuery, itself, does not.

00:29:14.580 --> 00:29:17.190
But you can, in a different
part of your program,

00:29:17.190 --> 00:29:21.570
you can call in API, and augment
the table that you're looking

00:29:21.570 --> 00:29:23.970
at with the geocoded data.

00:29:23.970 --> 00:29:29.100
Or you can call in the external
API as part of a Dataflow job.

00:29:29.100 --> 00:29:32.670
So you can read out a BigQuery
column through a Dataflow job,

00:29:32.670 --> 00:29:34.740
then call that API,
augment the data,

00:29:34.740 --> 00:29:36.725
and then write that
back into BigQuery.

00:29:36.725 --> 00:29:37.090
SOLEIL KELLEY: Got it.

00:29:37.090 --> 00:29:37.590
I see.

00:29:37.590 --> 00:29:38.850
Great.

00:29:38.850 --> 00:29:41.806
"For visualizations and
mapping, what other BI tools

00:29:41.806 --> 00:29:42.930
can I connect BigQuery to?"

00:29:42.930 --> 00:29:46.720
So, BigQuery he has a
number of native connections

00:29:46.720 --> 00:29:50.730
to different BI tools
like Tableau and Looker

00:29:50.730 --> 00:29:54.000
and Click and things
like this specifically

00:29:54.000 --> 00:29:57.980
for mapping and connecting to
your BigQuery GIS functions.

00:29:57.980 --> 00:30:01.870
So long as those tools
support custom SQL queries,

00:30:01.870 --> 00:30:04.590
and so long as they can
render geographic data types,

00:30:04.590 --> 00:30:08.380
they should be able to
leverage this technology.

00:30:08.380 --> 00:30:10.380
And one thing, as
well, is you probably

00:30:10.380 --> 00:30:14.650
want to bring that into GeoJSON
format, right, to do that?

00:30:14.650 --> 00:30:15.510
CHAD JENNINGS: Yeah.

00:30:15.510 --> 00:30:20.040
So Tableau is an
example of a Viz tool

00:30:20.040 --> 00:30:23.250
that you'll want to use the
custom queries to leverage

00:30:23.250 --> 00:30:24.870
BigQuery GIS.

00:30:24.870 --> 00:30:29.142
And then Looker actually
supports BigQuery GIS.

00:30:29.142 --> 00:30:30.470
SOLEIL KELLEY: OK, wonderful.

00:30:30.470 --> 00:30:31.310
Next question.

00:30:31.310 --> 00:30:33.720
"Does BigQuery GIS
support 3D geometries

00:30:33.720 --> 00:30:36.894
