to the BigQuery
documentation for GIS.

00:32:07.292 --> 00:32:08.750
There are a ton of
resources there.

00:32:08.750 --> 00:32:11.675
Again, it's all fully outlined--
all of the different functions

00:32:11.675 --> 00:32:12.300
that are there.

00:32:12.300 --> 00:32:14.210
There are several
tutorials as well.

00:32:14.210 --> 00:32:16.850
We'll post some links
to the Stack Overflow

00:32:16.850 --> 00:32:19.130
there as well unless
you want to add to that?

00:32:19.130 --> 00:32:21.080
CHAD JENNINGS: Yeah,
I do, definitely.

00:32:21.080 --> 00:32:25.730
So as we all know, we
work with geospatial data.

00:32:25.730 --> 00:32:27.560
And for those that
have, know that there

00:32:27.560 --> 00:32:30.830
are datasets all over the
place and in all sorts

00:32:30.830 --> 00:32:32.000
of different formats.

00:32:32.000 --> 00:32:34.492
And aside from
shapefiles, which is,

00:32:34.492 --> 00:32:36.200
I suppose, a bit of
an industry standard,

00:32:36.200 --> 00:32:38.480
but there's just like,
GDB, MDB-- there's

00:32:38.480 --> 00:32:40.190
a whole lot of stuff out there.

00:32:40.190 --> 00:32:42.290
We don't have a
SQL verb that says,

00:32:42.290 --> 00:32:45.020
like, go get this
dataset, and bring it in.

00:32:45.020 --> 00:32:47.450
However, it's a
really good idea,

00:32:47.450 --> 00:32:49.940
and we've already
written that down.

00:32:49.940 --> 00:32:52.790
But what you will have
to do is, if you're

00:32:52.790 --> 00:32:56.570
a non-programmer, have
a look at the resources,

00:32:56.570 --> 00:33:01.370
again, this article about
pulling in other types of data

00:33:01.370 --> 00:33:04.040
formats into BigQuery.

00:33:04.040 --> 00:33:08.057
And then the process to
copy that over requires

00:33:08.057 --> 00:33:09.140
a couple of lines of code.

00:33:09.140 --> 00:33:11.930
But you can literally copy
and paste from the article

00:33:11.930 --> 00:33:13.850
and put that into the console.

00:33:13.850 --> 00:33:15.860
And the directions
there are clear enough

00:33:15.860 --> 00:33:18.532
that even I was able to get it
right on the very first time.

00:33:18.532 --> 00:33:19.490
SOLEIL KELLEY: Amazing.

00:33:19.490 --> 00:33:21.156
CHAD JENNINGS: I was
not the author too,

00:33:21.156 --> 00:33:23.235
so I was actually testing.

00:33:23.235 --> 00:33:24.110
SOLEIL KELLEY: Great.

00:33:24.110 --> 00:33:25.068
Well, thanks everybody.

